  "query_preview": "/Friday through Tuesday/...\nIssued 401 AM MDT TUE OCT 20 2026\nThe low pressure system will be on the way out Friday as it\npasses overhead then NE and flow aloft shifts out of the \nnorthwest. The cold front will be through the area Friday \nmorning, bringing a cool down to around 5 degrees below normal. \nPrecipitation chances also clear out when it moves through. Of \nparticular concern for this front is the gusty west-northwest\nwinds. Friday afternoon, winds across ridge tops and open \nterrain will gust to 20- 35 mph. The strongest winds, however, \nwill blow in the Snake River Plain east of Mountain Home where \ngusts will reach 35-45 mph. Lowering snow levels on Friday \nbehind the front will allow for a transition to snow/wintry mix \nfor elevations above 6.5 kft MSL. This switch will be at the \ntail end of the precipitation, however, so only a dusting of \nsnow is expected along high elevation passes such as Banner \nSummit. \n\nPattern uncertainty increases behind this system, however\nSaturday at the very least should be dry with temperatures \nnear-normal again. Near-normal for this time of year does mean\nthat valley floors Saturday morning could see freezing to below\nfreezing temperatures. Cluster analysis for the past few days \nwas confident on upper-level troughing over the Pacific NW late \nthis weekend, however some model solutions are now introducing \nhigh amplitude upper-level ridging over the area. This stark \ndifference in synoptic pattern creates a large range of \nscenarios. At the time of this forecast, it does seem models are\nnow converging on keeping the trough offshore and upper- level \nridging building in.",
